1
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:08,200
Like many philosophers before him, Schopenhauer tried to understand reality, and it was an evil world he saw.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:15,900
Animal life seemed horrific;
Most creatures live on hunting, killing and devouring others -

3
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:19,220
Life was literally a struggle with beaks and claws.

4
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:24,620
His view of man was about the same;
violence and injustice everywhere,

5
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:29,120
Every single life was a meaningless tragedy that ends with death.

6
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:35,560
We live as slaves during our desires and life is a constant tour between suffering and boredom.

7
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:47,120
Arthur Schopenhauer was born in the German city of Danzig (present Gdańsk in Poland)

8
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:52,520
and has not surprisingly come to be regarded as the great pessimist among the philosophers.

9
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:58,460
To understand Schopenhauer, we must take our starting point in Immanuel Kant.

10
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:01,380
Schopenhauer held on the whole with edge,

11
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:04,840
But meant that he had made a couple of erroneous assumptions.

12
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,660
Schopenhauer tried to correct them and develop the theory.

13
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:16,500
Central to Kant's transcendental idealism is the division between the objective reality

14
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:23,460
.

15
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:27,540
Of these, only the latter we can have knowledge of.

16
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:31,800
We can never know what is beyond the human experience.

17
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:37,320
According to Kant we can never reach knowledge of the objective reality,

18
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:40,920
But at the same time, it must exist independently of us.

19
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:47,200
Something (Tinget-in-Sig) must give rise to what we experience (Tinget-for-Mig),

20
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:54,100
Otherwise, the conclusion is that our experiences of the world arise from nothing - which would be illogical.

21
00:01:54,700 --> 00:02:02,080
Furthermore, he meant that time, space and causality (causal relationship) exist only in the phenomenon world

22
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:06,560
as they are the consequences of how our brain and our consciousness work.

23
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:14,060
Here lies Kant's mistake, Schopenhauer said.
If time and space are only the consequences of our consciousness,

24
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:24,240
Without time and space, the objective reality must be non-material,

25
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:28,660
because physical matter cannot exist beyond these dimensions.

26
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:35,420
Another conclusion we can draw is that this non-material reality must be a singular.

27
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:40,460
For if you remove time and space, you remove the basic prerequisite for something

28
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:42,980
Being able to be separate from something else.

29
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:48,200
Even causality is bound to our experience of the world,

30
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:51,720
Thus, it is not something that exists in objective reality.

31
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:57,180
From this Schopenhauer concludes that we cannot possibly tell you that anything in the noumenon

32
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:00,120
Causes what we experience in the phenomenon world.

33
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:15,520
That we will never reach knowledge of the world by using our senses.

34
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:22,680
However, we can take advantage of the fact that there is one thing in the world we have direct knowledge of - our own body.

35
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:29,260
We see inward and try to peel away the physical body (as it exists only in the phenomenon of phenomena)

36
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:33,820
we find an internal operation characterized by a restless endeavor.

37
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:39,080
This operational force is at its most tangible during the sexual act or when our

38
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:45,820
Survival instincts are activated, Schopenhauer calls the "will to live" or just "the will".

39
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:50,260
All forms of life strive for reproduction and survival.

40
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:57,640
Schopenhauer therefore concludes that all life forms, like us, are manifestations of "the desire to live".

41
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:02,840
There was also no fundamental difference between what we call organic life

42
00:05:02,840 --> 00:05:07,600
(animals and plants) and the inorganic (non-living ting).

43
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:12,880
Everything is manifestations of the will, and everything is basically one with everything else.

44
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:17,120
The will drives us to constantly chase goals and dreams.

45
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:22,800
We believe that we will be happy if we achieve what we are looking for, but soon the joy will have disappeared

46
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:27,140
And the will has been aiming for new dreams and goals.
That's life.

47
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:32,960
The will drives us further through the belief that we can achieve lasting happiness and satisfaction.

48
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:38,120
When we strive for something, we do it based on a state of deficiency - we lack something.

49
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:44,000
We are dissatisfied with the current situation and long for something that is not yet ours.

50
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:49,180
The volatile joy we can sometimes experience when we have achieved a goal is according to Schopenhauer

51
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:54,960
Not positive but negative as it indicates the transition from one deficiency to another.

52
00:06:35,780 --> 00:06:42,400
An important source of inspiration for Schopenhauer is the "Upanishads", one of Hinduism's holy writings,

53
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:48,180
Why his philosophy can be seen as a mix between Western and Eastern philosophy.

54
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:55,200
In principle, he agreed with Hinduism and Buddhism but did not believe in karma, reincarnation or Nirvana.

55
00:06:55,940 --> 00:07:01,760
The similarities become clear if we compare Schopenhauer's philosophy with the "four truths of Buddhism"

56
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:06,820
which deals with the cause of suffering and suspension.
They read as follows:

57
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:09,260
There is suffering in the world.

58
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:11,980
The reason for the suffering is the desire.

59
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:14,860
The desire can and must be wiped out.

60
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:19,160
The road to extinguishing the desire is the eight -fold road.

61
